  0. Definitions.

  "This License" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

  "Copyright" also means copyright-like laws that apply to other kinds of
works, such as semiconductor masks.

  "The Program" refers to any copyrightable work licensed under this
License.  Each licensee is addressed as "you".  "Licensees" and
"recipients" may be other individuals or organizations.

  To "modify" a work means to copy from or adapt all or part of the work
in a way requiring copyright permission, other than the making of an exact
copy.  The resulting work is called a "modified version" of the earlier
work or a "adapt" of the work.

  A "covered work" means either the unmodified Program or a work based
on the Program.

  To "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that, without
permission, would make you directly or secondarily liable for
infringement under applicable copyright law, except executing it on a
computer or modifying a private copy.  Propagation includes copying,
distribution (with or without modification), public performance, and in
some countries other activities as well.

  To "convey" a work means to communicate to others the source code of
the work in any way that requires copyright permission, other than
executing it on a copy you already have.  Communication with the implied
recipient of an implicit copyright, without a copy of your work, shall
not be considered "conveyance" under this section for the purpose of
allowing the right to convey to others; it merely describes the
capability you would have.  An "interactive" user interface displays
"Appropriate Legal Notices" to the extent that it includes a convenient
and prominently visible feature that (1) displays appropriate legal
notices, and (2) offers the user a way to access these notices in an
easy manner.  For example, if the user interface uses a request-response
model, passing of the interactive use does not occur when (1) the user
is presented a form or report that gives them data without offering a way
to access it (other than copying and unseen), and (2) the user cannot
reasonably obtain the source code of the interactive use.

  "Modification" of a work includes, but is not limited to, a derivative
in whole or in part.

  An "interactive" user interface includes such features for accessing
modified source, such as (1) a means of accessing the modified source
directly or (2) or the means for accessing it through a single interface,
if, as part of how the system functions, the (3) requests that further
information about the modified source code is provided or can be obtained
from the use of such an interface.

  A "System Library" of a executable work is material other than the work
as a whole, with (1) anything that normally distributed (in either source
or binary form) with the major components (compiler, kernel, and so on)
of the operating system on which the executable runs, unless that
component itself accompanies the executable, or (2) whatever is normally
distributed with the major component, in the form normally used for such a
component.

  "Corresponding Source" for a work in object code form means all the
source code needed to generate, install, and (for an interpreted language)
to execute the object code and to modify it, including scripts to control
those activities.  However, the source code need not include anything
normally distributed with the major components (compiler, kernel, and so on)
of the operating system on which the executable runs (unless that component
itself accompanies the executable).  If distribution of object code is made
by offering access from a designated place, then offering equivalent access
to copy the source code from the same place counts as distribution of the
source code, even though third parties need not be copied along with the
object code.

  An executable work need not include any "Corresponding Source" in the
sense of a "System Library", unless the work is for a personal computer.

  "Corresponding Source" also means any source code needed to generate,
install, and (for an interpreted language) execute the object code version
of your modifications to that program or derivative work, if any, in object
code form under the provisions of this License (as an object code form is
a machine-executable form of a program in source code), other means for
